-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I14 (S1-CHAIN, #1380): the shadowed interface is nameable in this
-- module ONLY THROUGH A RE-EXPORT CHAIN.  `Sizeable` is not declared here, not the prelude,
-- and `ifc` is not imported here -- it arrives via `reexp`, which re-exports it by TYPE
-- name.  §1.0's `nameable in M` covers exactly that ("exported by a module `M` imports,
-- directly, OR THROUGH A RE-EXPORT CHAIN"), so `size` IS a shadow here, `Box` has a live
-- impl, and the occurrence dispatches: 300.
--
-- ⚠️ PAIRED WITH `direct.mdk`, WHICH THIS GATE ALSO RUNS AS ITS OWN ROW. That file is this
-- one with `import reexp.{Sizeable}` replaced by `import ifc.{Sizeable}` -- the SAME
-- interface reached directly instead of through the chain. The two must agree, and that
-- invariant holds under either reading of the clause, which is what makes it the thing to
-- code against rather than the value.
--
-- WHY THIS CELL EXISTS: the corpus contained ZERO `export import` before it (derive:
-- `grep -rl 'export import' test/shadow_fixtures/`), and the first cut of the #1353 fix
-- built its nameable-in-`M` predicate out of a METHOD-name index whose re-export fold
-- matches member lists against METHOD names. `export import ifc.{Sizeable}` names no
-- method, so the predicate answered "not nameable" and this program was REJECTED while
-- `direct.mdk` printed 300 -- a false reject on a program the base accepts, and the
-- non-conformance S1-CHAIN enumerates by name ("MAY NOT fail closed on chains").
import reexp.{Sizeable}
import boxmod.{Box(..)}
import fmod.{size}

main = println (size (Box 3))
